  "sections": [
    {
      "section_id": "defibrillator_section_1",
      "section_title": "Defibrillator and automated external defibrillator (AED)",
      "section_order": 1,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "subsection_id": "defibrillator_subsection_1",
          "subsection_title": "Overview",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "defibrillator_group_1",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/defibrillator_01.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/defibrillator_02.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A defibrillator delivers a controlled electrical shock to the heart when indicated, to terminate certain life-threatening arrhythmias and allow an organised rhythm to resume."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The automated external defibrillator (AED) analyses the cardiac rhythm and advises or delivers a shock when appropriate. It is designed to guide users through cardiac arrest management."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "This is a high-yield OSCE station because examiners commonly test identification, differences between manual defibrillators and AEDs, pad placement, shockable rhythms, electrical safety, and the sequence of actions during cardiac arrest."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Key principle: Defibrillation is indicated for ventricular fibrillation (VF) and pulseless ventricular tachycardia (pVT). It is not indicated for asystole or pulseless electrical activity (PEA)."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "defibrillator_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "1. Types of defibrillators",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "defibrillator_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/defibrillator_03.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A. Manual defibrillator"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Allows a trained clinician to interpret the rhythm, select the energy setting, and deliver a shock when indicated. Often includes ECG monitoring and synchronised cardioversion functions."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/defibrillator_01.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "B. Automated external defibrillator (AED)"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Analyses the rhythm and provides voice or visual instructions. It advises a shock only when its analysis identifies a shockable rhythm."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/defibrillator_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "C. Monitor-defibrillator"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Combines cardiac monitoring with defibrillation and may provide synchronised cardioversion and transcutaneous pacing, depending on the model."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "defibrillator_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "2. Components and functions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "defibrillator_group_3",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/defibrillator_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "1. Monitor and control panel"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Displays the cardiac rhythm and provides controls for selecting modes and energy on manual devices."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/defibrillator_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "2. Defibrillation pads or paddles"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Transfer the electrical energy through the chest. Adhesive pads are commonly used with AEDs and modern manual defibrillators."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/defibrillator_07.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "3. Connecting cable"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Connects the pads to the device and allows rhythm analysis and shock delivery."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/defibrillator_08.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "4. Energy selector and shock button"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "On a manual device, these allow a trained operator to select the prescribed energy and deliver a shock."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/defibrillator_09.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "5. Voice prompts and indicators"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Guide the operator through pad placement, rhythm analysis, shock decisions, and CPR prompts on an AED."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "defibrillator_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "3. Principle of defibrillation",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "defibrillator_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A defibrillator delivers a brief electrical current through the myocardium. In a shockable rhythm, this depolarises a critical mass of cardiac muscle, interrupting the disorganised electrical activity and giving the heart's natural pacemaker an opportunity to re-establish an organised rhythm."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Important: A shock does not guarantee that the heart will restart. CPR and ongoing resuscitation remain essential."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "defibrillator_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "4. Indications and shockable rhythms",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "defibrillator_group_5",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/defibrillator_10.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A. Ventricular fibrillation (VF)"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Chaotic electrical activity without effective coordinated ventricular contraction. In a pulseless patient, this is a shockable rhythm."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/defibrillator_11.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "B. Pulseless ventricular tachycardia (pVT)"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Rapid ventricular rhythm with no detectable pulse. This is shockable."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "defibrillator_group_6",
              "group_title": "Non-shockable rhythms",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/defibrillator_12.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Asystole"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No discernible ventricular electrical activity. Confirm the tracing and connections, continue CPR, and follow the cardiac arrest algorithm. Do not defibrillate asystole."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/defibrillator_13.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Pulseless electrical activity (PEA)"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Electrical activity is present, but there is no effective circulation. Continue CPR and treat reversible causes; do not defibrillate PEA."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Memory aid: VF and pulseless VT = shock. Asystole and PEA = no shock."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A patient with ventricular tachycardia who still has a pulse may need synchronised cardioversion if unstable, rather than unsynchronised defibrillation. This is a different clinical situation."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "defibrillator_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "5. How to position defibrillator pads",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "defibrillator_group_7",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/defibrillator_02.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/defibrillator_14.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/defibrillator_15.webp",
                  "caption": null
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "defibrillator_group_8",
              "group_title": "Standard adult anterior-lateral placement",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Right upper pad: Below the right clavicle, to the right of the sternum.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Left lateral pad: On the left side of the chest, below the armpit in the midaxillary region, approximately level with V6.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The pads should be placed on bare, dry skin, with good contact and without overlapping."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "defibrillator_group_9",
              "group_title": "Alternative: anterior-posterior placement",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "One pad is placed on the front of the chest and the other on the back. This may be used when anterior-lateral placement is unsuitable or when indicated by the clinical protocol."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "defibrillator_group_10",
              "group_title": "Precautions during pad placement",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Remove clothing from the pad sites.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Dry the chest if wet.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Avoid placing pads directly over an implanted pacemaker or defibrillator; position them away from the device when possible.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Follow pad instructions for patients with medication patches: remove a patch from the intended pad site using appropriate precautions and clean the skin.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "For children, use appropriate pediatric pads or an attenuating system when available, following device instructions.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "defibrillator_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "6. How to use an AED — practical OSCE sequence",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "defibrillator_group_11",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/defibrillator_16.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/defibrillator_17.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/defibrillator_18.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Recognise cardiac arrest: Check responsiveness and normal breathing. Activate emergency help and obtain an AED.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Start CPR: Begin high-quality chest compressions while the AED is being brought and prepared.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Switch on the AED: Follow the voice and visual instructions.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Expose and prepare the chest: Dry it if necessary.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Apply the pads: Use the illustrated pad positions.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Connect the pads: Attach the cable if it is not already connected.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Allow rhythm analysis: Ensure nobody touches the patient during analysis.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "7."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Deliver a shock if advised: Loudly announce that everyone must stand clear, visually confirm that nobody is touching the patient, and follow the AED instructions.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "8."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Resume CPR immediately: After a shock, resume chest compressions without delaying to check the pulse unless signs of life appear or the device directs otherwise.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "9."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Continue following prompts: Repeat analysis at the instructed intervals and continue resuscitation until the patient shows signs of life, trained help takes over, or the resuscitation team directs otherwise.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "10."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "OSCE safety phrase: “Everybody stand clear — I am clear, you are clear, everybody is clear.”"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Do not touch the patient during shock delivery."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "defibrillator_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "7. Manual defibrillator: key differences",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "defibrillator_group_12",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Feature",
                    "AED",
                    "Manual defibrillator"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Rhythm interpretation",
                      "Device analyses and advises",
                      "Trained clinician interprets"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Energy selection",
                      "Device determines shock recommendation and settings",
                      "Clinician selects energy according to rhythm and protocol"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Shock delivery",
                      "Operator follows device prompts",
                      "Trained operator delivers shock"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Synchronised cardioversion",
                      "Usually not available",
                      "Commonly available"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Typical users",
                      "Trained members of the public and healthcare workers",
                      "Trained clinical professionals"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Manual defibrillation settings vary by waveform, device, rhythm, patient age, and current resuscitation guidance. In an OSCE, state that you would use the manufacturer's recommendations and the applicable resuscitation protocol rather than memorising a single energy setting for every device."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "defibrillator_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "8. Precautions and complications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "defibrillator_group_13",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Risk",
                    "Prevention"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Electrical injury to rescuers",
                      "Ensure everyone is clear before shock delivery"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Burns or skin injury",
                      "Correct pad placement and good skin contact"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Ineffective shock",
                      "Use appropriate pads, positioning, and device settings"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Delayed CPR",
                      "Minimise interruptions in chest compressions"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Accidental shock",
                      "Clearly announce the shock and visually check that nobody is touching the patient"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Fire risk",
                      "Avoid flammable oxygen-enriched environments and follow local safety procedures"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Incorrect rhythm treatment",
                      "Correctly distinguish shockable from non-shockable rhythms"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Supplemental oxygen should be managed so that oxygen is not flowing freely across the chest during shock delivery. Follow the resuscitation team's oxygen and fire-safety procedures."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "defibrillator_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "9. Pediatric considerations",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "defibrillator_group_14",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/defibrillator_19.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/defibrillator_20.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/defibrillator_21.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Use pediatric pads or an energy attenuator when available and appropriate for the child's age and size.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "If pediatric equipment is unavailable, follow the relevant resuscitation guideline for use of an available AED rather than delaying lifesaving defibrillation in a child with cardiac arrest.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Pad placement must avoid overlap; use an anterior-posterior arrangement if the pads would otherwise touch.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Pediatric cardiac arrest management includes high-quality CPR, ventilation, rhythm assessment, and treatment of reversible causes.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "In newborn resuscitation at birth, follow the neonatal resuscitation algorithm rather than the standard AED sequence.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "defibrillator_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "10. Mnemonic: SHOCK",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "defibrillator_group_15",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "S"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Switch on the device and summon help."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "H"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Hook up the pads in the correct positions."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "O"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Observe the rhythm analysis without touching the patient."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "C"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Clear everyone before shock delivery."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "K"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Keep CPR going immediately after the shock or no-shock decision."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "defibrillator_subsection_12",
          "subsection_title": "11. Common OSCE examiner questions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "defibrillator_group_16",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. Which rhythms are shockable?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Ventricular fibrillation and pulseless ventricular tachycardia."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. Which rhythms are non-shockable?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Asystole and pulseless electrical activity."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. Where do you place adult defibrillator pads?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Usually in the anterior-lateral position: below the right clavicle and on the left lateral chest below the armpit."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. Why must nobody touch the patient during shock delivery?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "To prevent accidental electrical injury and ensure safe shock delivery."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. What should you do immediately after a shock?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Resume CPR immediately, beginning with chest compressions, and follow the resuscitation algorithm."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. What is the difference between defibrillation and synchronised cardioversion?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Defibrillation delivers an unsynchronised shock, usually for VF or pulseless VT. Synchronised cardioversion times the shock with the R wave and is used for selected tachyarrhythmias when indicated."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. Can you shock asystole?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. Continue CPR, give indicated medications under the relevant protocol, and treat reversible causes."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "defibrillator_subsection_13",
          "subsection_title": "12. Mini OSCE station",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "defibrillator_group_17",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Scenario: An adult collapses, is unresponsive, and is not breathing normally. You have access to an AED."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "Expected approach:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Confirm unresponsiveness and abnormal breathing.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Activate emergency help and begin CPR.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Obtain and switch on the AED.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Expose and prepare the chest, then attach the pads.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Ensure everyone is clear during rhythm analysis.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Deliver a shock if advised, after a clear safety check.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Resume CPR immediately and follow the AED prompts.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "7."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Examiner's key point: The AED must not delay CPR, and a shock must never be delivered while someone is touching the patient."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}